---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import SvgFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/SvgFigure.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
import { series, stroke } from "~/data/palette"

const ships = [25, 282]
const stringStart = ships[0] + 45
const stringEnd = ships[1]
const shorterEnd = 214
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="bells-spaceship-paradox"
  lead="The rockets keep the same gap. How can the string between them stretch?"
>
  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    Imagine watching two rockets from where they launched. They are connected by a
    taut string and will speed up together, so the distance between them stays fixed
    in your measurements.
  </p>

  <Panel title="1. Before accelerating" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <SvgFigure
      name="bell-before"
      class="mx-auto w-full max-w-md"
      viewBox="0 0 360 112"
      title="At rest: the string fits the distance between the two rockets"
      desc="Two blue rockets sit side by side, joined by a gold string. A bracket marks the distance between them. The string fits without stretching."
    >
      <path
        d={`M${stringStart} 39 H${stringEnd}`}
        stroke={series.gold}
        stroke-width="5"
        stroke-linecap="round"
      />
      {ships.map((x) => (
        <g>
          <rect
            x={x}
            y="26"
            width="34"
            height="26"
            rx="3"
            fill={series.blue}
            fill-opacity=".25"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="2"
          />
          <path
            d={`M${x + 34} 29 L${x + 45} 39 L${x + 34} 49 Z`}
            fill={series.blue}
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="2"
          />
          <circle cx={x + 15} cy="39" r="4" fill={series.blue} />
        </g>
      ))}
      <path
        d={`M${stringStart} 68 V75 H${stringEnd} V68`}
        fill="none"
        stroke={stroke}
        stroke-width="1.5"
      />
      <text x="175" y="95" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle">
        Original gap = unstretched string
      </text>
    </SvgFigure>
    <p class="text-base leading-normal">The gold string fits the gap exactly.</p>
  </Panel>

  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    <strong>One rule of relativity:</strong> a fast-moving object is measured shorter
    along the direction it travels than when it is at rest, provided it isn't
    stretched. This is called <em>length contraction</em>.
  </p>

  <Panel title="2. After accelerating" accent="red" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <p class="text-base leading-normal">
      The rockets have travelled, but their gap is still the same. The actual string
      has to reach between them.
    </p>
    <SvgFigure
      name="bell-after"
      class="mx-auto w-full max-w-md"
      viewBox="0 0 360 187"
      title="The gap remains the same, but an unstretched moving string would be shorter"
      desc="The two rockets are drawn at the same separation as before. A red string spans the unchanged gap. Below, a gold line shows the shorter length an unstretched moving string would have as measured from the launch site, aligned with the rear rocket. A red double-headed arrow shows the additional length needed to reach the front rocket."
    >
      <path
        d={`M${stringStart} 34 H${stringEnd}`}
        stroke={series.red}
        stroke-width="5"
        stroke-linecap="round"
      />
      {ships.map((x) => (
        <g>
          <rect
            x={x}
            y="21"
            width="34"
            height="26"
            rx="3"
            fill={series.blue}
            fill-opacity=".25"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="2"
          />
          <path
            d={`M${x + 34} 24 L${x + 45} 34 L${x + 34} 44 Z`}
            fill={series.blue}
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="2"
          />
          <circle cx={x + 15} cy="34" r="4" fill={series.blue} />
        </g>
      ))}
      <path
        d={`M${stringStart} 61 V68 H${stringEnd} V61`}
        fill="none"
        stroke={stroke}
        stroke-width="1.5"
      />
      <text x="175" y="87" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle">
        Same gap as before
      </text>
      <text x="9" y="114" font-size="14" fill="currentColor">
        If the moving string were not stretched:
      </text>
      <path
        d={`M${stringStart} 133 H${shorterEnd}`}
        stroke={series.gold}
        stroke-width="5"
        stroke-linecap="round"
      />
      <path
        d={`M${shorterEnd + 4} 133 H${stringEnd - 4} M${shorterEnd + 11} 127 L${shorterEnd + 4} 133 L${shorterEnd + 11} 139 M${stringEnd - 11} 127 L${stringEnd - 4} 133 L${stringEnd - 11} 139`}
        stroke={series.red}
        stroke-width="2"
        fill="none"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      />
      <text x="141" y="158" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle">
        Shorter string
      </text>
      <text
        x="249"
        y="158"
        font-size="14"
        font-weight="700"
        fill="currentColor"
        text-anchor="middle"
      >
        Extra stretch
      </text>
    </SvgFigure>
    <p class="text-base leading-normal">
      If the string stayed unstretched, it would not reach the front rocket.
      To span the unchanged gap, the real string must stretch.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <p class="text-base font-bold">
    Same gap, but a stretched string. If the rockets keep accelerating, the strain
    eventually breaks it.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="caption">
    Both drawings use measurements made from the launch site. The rockets are
    repositioned in the picture to compare their gap; their real positions do move.
    The shortening and stretching are exaggerated. How soon a string breaks depends
    on its strength, and a small increase in speed need not snap it.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
